{"type":"target-event","projectId":"000458-native-ch19-seq102","event":{"type":"proposal","proposal":{"summary":"Revise ch19-seq102 into a source-grounded English reflection with a clearer hook, a developed causal bridge, and a short settle; keep roles separate and preserve the excerpt’s qualifiers and concrete anchors.","command":{"projectId":"000458-native-ch19-seq102","expectedRevision":1,"idempotencyKey":"0cf7d00c-4b2e-4aa1-96b9-19ce83390ace","operations":[{"kind":"set-metadata","title":"ch19-seq102 — recovered editorial reflection","language":"en","durationInFrames":2700},{"kind":"set-text","itemId":"reflection","text":"This story is repeated in Matthew, Mark, and Luke, and that repeated witness tells us not to rush past it. When three inspired writers keep the same storm before us, we should give the more heed to what it shows about disciples under pressure. The lake turns violent, the wind drives down, the boat takes in water, and true disciples still cry, \"Master, Master, we are going to perish!\" That fear is humbling, because it exposes how quickly sight, sense, and feeling can make men poor theologians. Even loyal believers can forget, for a moment, their Master's never-failing care, and forget that with Him they were safe whatever happened. The lesson is not that grace was absent, but that faith was not ready for use. We need to watch and pray against that weakness in ourselves, and to be moderate in what we expect from other Christians. Men may be believers, and still be overwhelmed with fears. That is why the question, \"Where is your faith?\" matters so much. Faith must be more than something we can name in sunshine; it must be kept active enough for the storm."},{"kind":"set-text","itemId":"bridge","text":"And that is the causal step the storm teaches: if trusting Christ is real, then it has to be ready before the wind rises, not improvised after the boat is filling. The point is not to shame weakness into silence. It is to see that the highest style of Christian lives by a faith exercised in need, seeing Him who is invisible when the water is breaking over the side."},{"kind":"set-text","itemId":"settle","text":"Take a quiet breath. Let that question remain."},{"kind":"set-timing","itemId":"reflection","durationInFrames":1080,"timingLocked":true},{"kind":"set-timing","itemId":"bridge","durationInFrames":960,"timingLocked":true},{"kind":"set-timing","itemId":"settle","durationInFrames":660,"timingLocked":true}]},"quality":{"coverage":[{"role":"reflection","status":"present","itemIds":["reflection"]},{"role":"bridge","status":"present","itemIds":["bridge"]},{"role":"settle","status":"present","itemIds":["settle"]}],"findings":[{"criterion":"coherence","status":"pass","reason":"The script opens with the repeated-witness hook, moves causally from the storm scene to the disciples’ fear, then explains why faith must be ready before need, ending with a short settle that leaves the question lingering.","itemIds":["reflection","bridge","settle"],"sources":[{"assetId":"707c7793-b13d-4334-b9aa-69c9ebbbd9e8","versionId":"cmtrhwiqn00032cvbgmw3rn56","digest":"566b8193ee115a73c8893c05dd1c10de1eef954440b7acccacd9e66605c7a588"}]},{"criterion":"depth","status":"pass","reason":"The adaptation retains the excerpt’s developed reasoning: repeated Gospel attestation, the humbling effect of fear in true disciples, the warning about judging other Christians, and the distinction between possessing faith and having it ready for use.","itemIds":["reflection","bridge"],"sources":[{"assetId":"707c7793-b13d-4334-b9aa-69c9ebbbd9e8","versionId":"cmtrhwiqn00032cvbgmw3rn56","digest":"566b8193ee115a73c8893c05dd1c10de1eef954440b7acccacd9e66605c7a588"}]},{"criterion":"fidelity","status":"pass","reason":"The script preserves the excerpt’s core qualifiers and anchors without inventing new facts: the event is repeated in three Gospels, the storm fills the boat, the disciples cry out, fear makes humans poor theologians, and faith must be active in the time of need.","itemIds":["reflection","bridge"],"sources":[{"assetId":"707c7793-b13d-4334-b9aa-69c9ebbbd9e8","versionId":"cmtrhwiqn00032cvbgmw3rn56","digest":"566b8193ee115a73c8893c05dd1c10de1eef954440b7acccacd9e66605c7a588"}]},{"criterion":"voice_command_vs_appeal","status":"pass","reason":"The script avoids directive pressure toward the viewer; the only imperative is the short settle invitation, which functions as a neutral release rather than a coercive command.","itemIds":["settle"],"sources":[]},{"criterion":"voice_third_party_address","status":"pass","reason":"It does not drift into speaking about a stranger’s prospects; the language stays on the disciples, the reader’s caution, and the faith lesson.","itemIds":["reflection","bridge"],"sources":[]},{"criterion":"voice_self_echo","status":"pass","reason":"The reflection does not repeat the same proposition without development. Repeated terms like faith, storm, and fear are advanced into a new causal point about readiness for use.","itemIds":["reflection","bridge"],"sources":[]},{"criterion":"voice_opening_recap","status":"pass","reason":"The opening begins with the editorial hook about repeated Gospel witness rather than a scene-by-scene recap that delays the argument.","itemIds":["reflection"],"sources":[]},{"criterion":"voice_scripture_echo","status":"not_checked","reason":"No verse-card or canonical subtitle context was supplied for exact redundant-quotation comparison, so a scripture-echo verdict cannot be verified here.","itemIds":["reflection","bridge"],"sources":[{"assetId":"707c7793-b13d-4334-b9aa-69c9ebbbd9e8","versionId":"cmtrhwiqn00032cvbgmw3rn56","digest":"566b8193ee115a73c8893c05dd1c10de1eef954440b7acccacd9e66605c7a588"}]}]}}}}
